1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Lorenzo di Piero de' Medici (Italian: [lo'r?ntso di 'pj??ro de 'm??dit?i]; 12 September 1492 – 4 May 1519) was the ruler of Florence from 1516 until his death in 1519. He was also Duke of Urbino during the same 
2 CONC period. His daughter''' Catherine de' Medici became Queen Consort of France, while '''his illegitimate son''', Alessandro de' Medici, became the first Duke of Florence.
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT '''Lorenzo was born in Florence on 12 September 1492''', a son of Piero di Lorenzo de' Medici and Alfonsina Orsini.[2] '''His paternal grandparents''' were Lorenzo the Magnificent and Clarice Orsini.[2] '''His maternal grandparent
2 CONC s''' were Roberto Orsini, Count of Tagliacozzo and his wife, Catherine of San Severino.
2 CONT Career
2 CONT '''Lorenzo II became lord of Florence in August 1513, after his uncle, Giuliano de' Medici, handed over control of its government. Ambitious by nature, Lorenzo II, despite being appointed Captain of the Florentine militia, lacked 
2 CONC patience with Florence's republican system of government and thus, in 1516, convinced his uncle, Pope Leo X to make him Duke of Urbino at the age of 24.[3] So began a conflict with the city's previous duke, Francesco Maria I della
2 CONC  Rovere. During the protracted War of Urbino, Delle Rovere recaptured the city, only to have Medici — commanding a 10,000-man Papal army — in turn, retake the city. During battle, Lorenzo was wounded, which prompted him to retire 
2 CONC to Tuscany. In September 1517, he regained Urbino via treaty; however, it remained under the Medici family's rule for only two years'''. In 1521 the duchy reverted to the Della Rovere family.[3]
2 CONT '''On 13 June 1518, Lorenzo married Madeleine de la Tour''', daughter of the Count of Auvergne.[4] '''The marriage produced a daughter''', Catherine, in 1519. Catherine de' Medici went on to become Queen of France, via a marriage 
2 CONC to the future King Henry II of France, arranged by the second Medici Pope, Pope Clement VII.[5]
2 CONT Only 21 days after Catherine de' Medici's birth,''' Lorenzo II died, "worn out by disease and excess'''."[6] Thus his daughter Catherine was raised primarily by the Medici Popes, Leo X and Clement VII, and their surrogates.
2 CONT '''Lorenzo II's tomb is in the Medici Chapel of Florence's Church of San Lorenzo. There is disagreement over which of the two tombs is Lorenzo II's. The received view is that Lorenzo'ss tomb that is adorned by Michelangelo's sculp
2 CONC ture Pensieroso, which offers an idealized portrait of Lorenzo II, and that its companion piece, also sculpted by Michelangelo, represents Lorenzo II's uncle''' Giuliano di Lorenzo de' Medici. '''But historian Richard Trexler has 
2 CONC argued that Lorenzo II, having been Captain of the Florentine militia, must be the figure holding the baton, symbol of military authority conferred by the Republic. Trexler also notes that the "Pensieroso" is holding a mappa, the 
2 CONC symbol of military authority in ancient Rome''', which would be an appropriate symbol for Giuliano di Lorenzo, who was appointed Captain of Roman forces. '''In sharing the same name with his illustrious ancestor, Lorenzo the Magni
2 CONC ficent, the Duke's tomb is often mistaken for that of his grandfather'''.[7][8]
2 CONT Famously, Niccolò Machiavelli dedicated his political treatise The Prince to Lorenzo to advise him of tactics to use to maintain his authority.[9]
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lorenzo_de%27_Medici,_Duke_of_Urbino
2 CONT '''Lorenzo II de' Medici'''
2 CONT '''Ruler of Florence'''
2 CONT '''Reign	17 March 1516 – 4 May 1519'''
2 CONT '''Predecessor'''	Giuliano de' Medici
2 CONT '''Successor'''	Giulio de' Medici
2 CONT '''Born	12 September 1492'''
2 CONT '''Florence, Republic of Florence'''
2 CONT '''Died	4 May 1519 (aged 26)'''
2 CONT '''Careggi, Republic of Florence'''
2 CONT Noble family	Medici
2 CONT '''Spouse(s)	Madeleine de La Tour d'Auvergne'''
2 CONT '''Issue'''	Catherine, Queen of France
2 CONT Alessandro de' Medici, Duke of Florence (illegitimate)
2 CONT '''Father'''	Piero the Unfortunate
2 CONT '''Mother'''	Alfonsina Orsini
