1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Neri di Gino Capponi''' (Florence, July 3, 1388 – Florence, November 27, 1457) was an Italian politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neri_di_Gino_Capponi_(1388-1457) (Gemini AI translation)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in 1388 into the Florentine Capponi family, who were reasonably wealthy and influential in civic politics. He quickly distinguished himself as a skilled politician and astute strategist, always capable of maintaining a
2 CONC  certain balance among the various political factions. He was particularly concerned with the welfare of the state rather than that of one of the major families.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1433, he was tasked by the ''Opera del Duomo'' with commissioning Donatello—who had returned from a trip to Rome between 1431 and 1432—to create the famous ''cantoria'' (choir loft) above the door of the canons' sacristy in San
2 CONC ta Maria del Fiore. The work was completed in 1439 and has been preserved in the Museo dell'Opera del Duomo since May 3, 1891.
2 CONT
2 CONT A mediator between the Albizzi and Medici families, he recalled Cosimo de' Medici from exile. Although sometimes at odds with Cosimo, he led Florence to victory against Lucca (under Francesco Burlamacchi) in the wars of the 1430s 
2 CONC and was always a proponent of the alliance between Florence and Venice against the Visconti. In 1440, he participated in the Battle of Anghiari, contributing significantly to the victory of the allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the author of notable historical works. [citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT === Descendants ===
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Selvaggia di Filippo Sacchetti (d. September 15, 1450), with whom he had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Margherita''' (1415 – May 13, 1498), married Lorenzo di Bartolo Corsi (d. November 28, 1508)
2 CONT * '''Francesco''' (1416 – ?)
2 CONT * '''Tommaso''' (1417 – 1444)
2 CONT * '''Giovanni''' (1418 – 1433)
2 CONT * '''Cappone''' (1418 – ?), twin of Giovanni
2 CONT * '''Lapa''' (1421 – 1446), married Lorenzo di Parigi Corbinelli
2 CONT * '''Gino''' (January 22, 1423 – April 23, 1487), politician and sole paternal heir; married Maddalena di Raimondo Mannelli (d. January 17, 1491) in 1443 and was the father of Pier Capponi
2 CONT * '''Maddalena''' (1425 – 1459), married Bernardo di Gerozzo Bardi
2 CONT * '''Andrea''' (1427 – ?)
2 CONT * '''Nanna''' (c. 1430 – April 10, 1508), married Tanai di Francesco de' Nerli (1427–1498) in 1447
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/neri-capponi_%28Dizionario-Biografico%29/
2 CONT
2 CONT CAPPONI, Neri
2 CONT
2 CONT Born on July 3, 1388, to Gino di Neri and Margherita di Iacopo Nasi, he inherited from his father a preeminent position within the Florentine political class of the first half of the 15th century, and in the first two decades of t
2 CONC he Medici regime was second in importance only to Cosimo de' Medici.   ….
2 CONT
2 CONT … Capponi had married Selvaggia di Tommaso Sacchetti, who bore him ten children and died on September 15, 1450. Capponi's considerable wealth was inherited by his son Gino, with appropriate bequests for his surviving daughters, wh
2 CONC o were married to members of the Nerli, Corbinelli, Bardi, and Manetti families.
2 CONT
2 CONT To posterity, Capponi left those Commentarii which, as the chronicle of one of the most active and engaged political figures of the era, remain an invaluable source for the history of that period. Capponi's historical writings—nam
2 CONC ely the Commentarii and La cacciata dei conti di Poppi ("The Expulsion of the Counts of Poppi")—were edited by L. A. Muratori in Rerum Italicarum Scriptores, XVIII, Milan 1731, along with the Commentarii sull'acquisto di Pisa, whi
2 CONC ch are often attributed to Capponi (see the bibliography for Capponi's father, Gino).
2 CONT
2 CONT Capponi's portrait appears several times in Florentine painting and sculpture. The only authentic one is the medallion depicting him in advanced age on his tomb, in all probability executed by Bernardo Rossellino. In Ghirlandaio's
2 CONC  frescoes in the Sassetti Chapel in Santa Trinita (c. 1481–85), he appears instead at a younger age and can be recognized in the bare-headed figure on the right in the Resurrection of the Roman Notary's Son. This portrait likely d
2 CONC erives from the terracotta bust, now in the Bargello, often attributed to Donatello and erroneously identified with Niccolò da Uzzano. In the following century, Capponi was portrayed by Vasari in a Palazzo Vecchio fresco riding be
2 CONC hind Cosimo the Elder upon his return to Florence from exile. A series of frescoes representing the most important moments of his life was painted around 1585 by Bernardino Poccetti in the Palazzo Capponi on today's Lungarno Guicc
2 CONC iardini, where a profile portrait of him wearing a turban—derived from his tomb medallion—also exists.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Ghirlandaio and the Sassetti chapel V (Santa Trinita)
2 CONT 3.5 The Resurrection of the Notary’s Son
2 CONT https://www.teggelaar.com/ghirlandaio-and-the-sassetti-chapel-v-santa-trinita/
2 CONT
2 CONT The figure with the bald head next to Domenico Ghirlandaio is a posthumous portrait of Neri di Gino Capponi, who died in 1457. Capponi had a huge reputation as the hero of the Battle of Anghiari. Fonzio, a friend of Francesco Sass
2 CONC etti who conceived the epitaphs on the tombs, wrote a short biography of Capponi. His grandson and namesake was married to Violante, the daughter of Francesco and Nera. Capponi’s palace can be seen in the fresco, on the right acro
2 CONC ss from the Ponte Santa Trinita. The other four figures standing next to Capponi are presumably the fathers of the sons-in-law or fiancés.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Anghiari The Battle of Anghiari was fought on 29 June 1440, between the forces of Milan and the League of some Italian states led by the Republic of Florence in the course of the Wars in Lo
2 CONC mbardy. The battle was a victory for the Florentines, who secured their domination of central Italy.
2 CONT * https://www.wga.hu/html_m/r/rosselin/bernardo/capponi.html Tomb of Neri di Gino Capponi 1450s Marble Santo Spirito, Florence.  Neri di Gino Capponi (1388-1457) was a Florentine politician and historian. He acted as a moderator i
2 CONC n the struggles between the Albizzi and the Medici.
2 CONT * https://www.deutsche-digitale-bibliothek.de/item/6IADW3E5PMVF2ECAOJCCI5EO5B5GNUAZ
2 CONT * “Political Advice from a Medieval Statesman.”  < [https://www.medievalists.net/2024/09/political-advice-medieval-statesman/ Medievalists.net] > How should one govern a state? For answers, we can turn to the wisdom of Gino di Ner
2 CONC i Capponi, a 15th-century statesman whose advice on politics and governance remains surprisingly relevant. Written nearly 600 years ago, Capponi’s insights continue to offer valuable lessons for both modern leaders and individuals
2 CONC  alike.
