1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Zie [http://resources.huygens.knaw.nl/vrouwenlexicon/lemmata/data/Margaretha%20van%20Parma '''Huygens ING...''']
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_of_Parma
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret, Duchess of Parma (28 December 1522 – 18 January 1586), governor of the Netherlands from 1559 to 1567, was the illegitimate daughter of Charles V.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother, Johanna Maria van der Gheynst, a servant of Charles de Lalaing, Seigneur de Montigny, was a Fleming. Margaret was brought up by the Douwrin family, and later by her great-aunt, Archduchess Margaret of Austria, and her 
2 CONC aunt, Maria of Austria, who were successively governors of the Netherlands from 1507 to 1530 and from 1530 to 1555, respectively. In 1533 she was acknowledged by her father and allowed to assume the name Margaret of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1527, in the year she turned five, she became engaged to the Pope's nephew, Alexander de Medici, Duke of Florence. In 1533, she married her betrothed but he was assassinated in 1537.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Her doctor:  https://www.geni.com/people/Ettore-Scribani-nobile-italiano/6000000172505125119?through=6000000172505391926
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT Spouse 	Alessandro de' Medici
2 CONT
2 CONT Ottavio Farnese
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Farnese
2 CONT
2 CONT House 	House of Habsburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Johanna Maria van der Gheynst
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	28 December 1522(1522-12-28)
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	18 January 1586 (aged 63)
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Parma (Italian: Margherita di Parma; 28 December 1522 – 18 January 1586) was Governor of the Netherlands from 1559 to 1567 and from 1578 to 1582. She was the illegitimate daughter of the then 22-year-old Holy Roman Emp
2 CONC eror Charles V and Johanna Maria van der Gheynst. She was a Duchess of Florence and a Duchess of Parma and Piacenza by marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother, Johanna Maria van der Gheynst, a servant of Charles de Lalaing, Seigneur de Montigny, was a Fleming. Margaret was brought up by the Douwrin family, and later by her great-aunt, Archduchess Margaret of Austria, and her 
2 CONC aunt, Maria of Austria, who were successively governors of the Netherlands from 1507 to 1530 and from 1530 to 1555, respectively. In 1533 she was acknowledged by her father and allowed to assume the name Margaret of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1527, in the year she turned five, she became engaged to the Pope's nephew, Alexander de Medici, Duke of Florence. In 1533, she married her betrothed but he was assassinated in 1537. On 4 November 1538 she became the wife of Ot
2 CONC tavio Farnese, Duke of Parma, the grandson of Pope Paul III. He was thirteen years old, she sixteen. The union, which proved an unhappy one, produced twin sons, one of whom died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like her aunts, who trained her, Margaret was a woman with many abilities. Philip II acted wisely in appointing her governor of the Netherlands when he left them in 1559 for Spain. In ordinary times she would probably have proved 
2 CONC as successful a ruler as her two predecessors in that post, but her task was very different from theirs. She had to face the rising storm of discontent against the Inquisition and Spanish despotism, and Philip left her but nominal
2 CONC  authority. He was determined to pursue his own arbitrary course, and the issue was the revolt of the Netherlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1567 Margaret resigned her post into the hands of the duke of Alva and retired to Italy. She had the satisfaction of seeing her son Alexander Farnese appointed to the office she had laid down, and to watch his successful career
2 CONC  as governor-general of the Netherlands. She died in Ortona in 1586.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     * "What, madam, is your highness afraid of these beggars (ces gueux)?"
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the article "Margaret of Austria" in the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT MARGARET OF AUSTRIA  (1522-1586), duchess of Parma and regent of the Netherlands from 1559 to 1567, was a natural daughter of Charles V. Her mother, Margaret van Ghent, was a Fleming. She was brought up by her aunts Margaret of Au
2 CONC stria and Maria of Hungary, who were successively regents of the Netherlands from 1507 to 1530 and from 1S30 to 1555. In 1533 she was married to Alexander de' Medici, duke of Florence, who was assassinated in 1537, after which she
2 CONC  became the wife of Ottavio Farnese, duke of Parma, in 1542. The union proved an unhappy one. Like her aunts, who had trained her, she was a woman of masculine abilities, and Philip II., when he left the Netherlands in 1559 for Sp
2 CONC ain, acted wisely in appointing her regent. In ordinary times she would probably have proved as successful a ruler as her two predecessors in that post, but her task was very different from theirs. She had to face the rising storm
2 CONC   of discontent against the Inquisition and Spanish despotism, and Philip left her but nominal authority. He was determined to pursue his own arbitrary course, and the issue was the revolt of the Netherlands. In 1567 Margaret resi
2 CONC gned her post into the hands of the duke of Alva and retired to Italy. She had the satisfaction of seeing her son Alexander Farnese appointed to the office she had laid down, and to watch his successful career as governor-general 
2 CONC of the Netherlands. She died at Ortona in 1586.
2 CONT
