1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT - http://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/neri-acciaiuoli_res-8d083c90-87e5-11dc-8e9d-0016357eee51_(Dizionario-Biografico)/
2 CONT
2 CONT - http://www.genmarenostrum.com/pagine-lettere/letteraa/ACCIAIOLI2.htm - Duca di Atene e Signore di Megara, Sicione, Tebe e Livadia dal 1435, detronizzato dal fratello nel 1439, tornò al potere nel 1441 dopo la morte di questi, ri
2 CONC conobbe l’Alta Sovranità di Costantino Paleologo (futuro Basileus) nel febbraio 1444 e gli cedette Tebe, si offerse nel 1444 come tributario della Sublime Porta e riebbe nel 1446 Tebe per opera del Sultano Murad 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nerio_II_Acciaioli
2 CONT
2 CONT Nerio II Acciaioli (1416–1451) was the Duke of Athens on two separate occasions from 1435 to 1439 and again from 1441 to 1451. He was a member of the Acciaioli  family of Florence, the son of Francesco Acciaioli, Lord of Sykaminon
2 CONC . His rule was contemporaneous with a renewed Italian philhellenism and corresponding interest in antiquities and the Greek language. Nerio not only spoke Greek naturally, but also owned the most famous monuments of the Hellenic w
2 CONC orld in his capital of Athens. Nerio arrived in Greece in 1419 on the death of his father when he was only three years old. He was named heir to his uncle Antonio I of Athens, but on his uncle's death in 1435, he had to fight his 
2 CONC uncle's widow Maria Melissena and the Chalcocondylae for the ducal throne. Supported by Murad II, the Ottoman sultan, against the Greek Constantine Palaeologus, Despot of Morea. After securing his position with Turkish help, he wa
2 CONC s removed by the intrigues of his brother Antonio II and driven from the Acropolis. His inveterate personal enemy, the chronicler Laonicus Chalcocondylas, venomously denigrates him as "effeminate." Nerio returned to power in 1441 
2 CONC after spending a few short years in Florence. He immediately expelled his brother's widow Maria Zorzi. It is probably that Nerio was present when the Emperor John VIII made a proclamation of Catholicism in the Florentine Duomo on 
2 CONC 6 July 1439. In 1444, Nerio went to war against the Turks on the side of Constantine, but came to terms with the Ottomans. He subsequently lost Thebes to Constantine and was forced to pay him tribute and become his vassal. In 1446
2 CONC , Murad assisted Nerio in retaking Thebes for the Latins. On his death, he was succeeded by his young son Francesco under the regency of his widow Chiara Zorzi.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Duca di Atene e Signore di Megara, Sicione, Tebe e Livadia dal 1435, detronizzato dal fratello nel 1439, tornò al potere nel 1441 dopo la morte di questi, riconobbe l’Alta Sovranità di Costantino Paleologo (futuro Basileus) nel fe
2 CONC bbraio 1444 e gli cedette Tebe, si offerse nel 1444 come tributario della Sublime Porta e riebbe nel 1446 Tebe per opera del Sultano Murad
