1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/porzia-massimo_(Dizionario-Biografico)/
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Porzia Massimo''' was born, presumably in Rome, around 1540 to Luca Massimo and Virginia di Giulio Colonna, of the Roman branch of the Colonna family.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was given in marriage in 1553 to Giovan Battista Salviati, firstborn of Lorenzo and Costanza Conti, a young scion of the branch of the Florentine family who had arrived in Rome at the time of the first Medici papacy with Jacop
2 CONC o, brother-in-law of [https://www.geni.com/people/Papa-Leone-X/6000000001063289132  Leo X] having married his sister Lucrezia de' Medici, and who had successfully entered the aristocratic fabric of the city since the beginning of 
2 CONC the 1530s.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 October 1562, assisted by Filippo Neri, in Rome, Salviati died. Widowed at only 22 years of age, without her union with her husband having produced any children, M., with the encouragement of Caterina Ricci, decided shortly t
2 CONC hereafter to enter a convent. 
2 CONT
2 CONT M. took the Dominican habit on 25 March 1563 with the name of''' Maria Vittoria''' in the monastery of S. Lucia in Florence, where it seems that Salviati himself had expressed the desire that she enter after his death. In the mona
2 CONC stery, to which she donated a part of her assets and of which she quickly became prioress, undoubtedly thanks to the social weight of her origins but also by virtue of the personal esteem she was able to earn, M. remained for abou
2 CONC t a decade. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT The community of S. Caterina da Siena in Magnanapoli, refounded by M. and permanently established in October 1574, quickly became one of the largest in Rome; it acquired prestige and growing weight in the city. A destination for g
2 CONC irls from the most noble Roman families, the monastery saw its income and the numerical consistency of the community grow considerably (27 in 1574, the nuns were 100 in 1620), before facing, in the 1620s, expansions and major reno
2 CONC vation works, as well as the reconstruction of the church, which took on its current appearance.
2 CONT
2 CONT M. was prioress and chamberlain of the community on several occasions; there she was joined by her mother, who had already followed her to S. Lucia in Florence. She guided the life of the monastery until her death, giving proof of
2 CONC  rigor, piety and devotion and of a fair amount of culture.
2 CONT
2 CONT M. died in Rome on March 31, 1603. She was buried, like her husband, in [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Maria_sopra_Minerva ''S. Maria sopra Minerva''].
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a difference between the title of prioress and that of abbess: 
2 CONT the '''prioress''' must submit to the power of a male convent, which acts as her guardian, while the abbess, who is invested with something called abbatial dignity, is not subjected to a whole mania of...  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Priory 
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prior_(ecclesiastical)
2 CONT * https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chiesa_di_Santa_Caterina_a_Magnanapoli
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Caterina_a_Magnanapoli Santa Caterina a Magnanapoli is a baroque church dedicated to St. Catherine of Siena on Largo Magnanapoli on the slopes of the Quirinal Hill in Rome.
2 CONT * http://www.ordinariatomilitare.it/s-caterina-a-magnanapoli/
