2 CONT === Elizabeth de Valois
2 CONT F, #105247, b. 1545, d. 1568
2 CONT Last Edited=3 Dec 2008
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=0.03%
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth de Valois, Princesse de France was born in 1545. (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Henri II, Roi de France and Catherine de Medici. She married Felipe II von Habsburg, Rey de España, son of Karl V von Habsburg, Holy Roman Emperor and Isabel de Aviz, Infanta de Portugal, in 1559 at Palacio
2 CONC  del Infantado, Guadalajara, Spain. (3) 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in 1568. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth de Valois, Princesse de France gained the title of Princesse Elizabeth de France. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Elizabeth de Valois, Princesse de France and Felipe II von Habsburg, Rey de España:
2 CONT
2 CONT * -1. unnamed daughter1 Habsburg b. 1564
2 CONT * -2. unnamed daughter2 Habsburg b. 1564
2 CONT * -3. Isabella Clara Eugenia von Habsburg+ b. 1566, d. 1633
2 CONT * -4. Catalina Micaela von Habsburg+ b. 10 Oct 1567, d. 6 Nov 1597
2 CONT * -5. unnamed daughter3 Habsburg b. 1568, d. 1568
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT * http://thepeerage.com/p10525.htm#i105247 cites
2 CONT * 1.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 161. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family. 
2 CONT * 2.[S130] Wikipedia, online http;//www.wikipedia.org. Hereinafter cited as Wikipedia. 
2 CONT * 3.[S3409] Caroline Maubois, "re: Penancoet Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 2 December 2008. Hereinafter cited as "re: Penancoet Family."
2 CONT * 4.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family, page 161, says Toledo.
2 CONT  --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Elisabeth of Valois''' From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elisabeth_of_Valois Wikipedia], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth de Valois (April 2, 1545 – October 3, 1568) was the eldest daughter of Henry II of France and Catherine de' Medici.She was born in the Chateau of Fontainebleu. Her childhood was spent mostly in the company of her future 
2 CONC sister-in-law, Mary, Queen of Scots, who was being raised at the French court. Elisabeth was described as being shy, timid and very much in awe of her formidable mother; although there is also evidence which shows Catherine to hav
2 CONC e been loving and tender toward Elisabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT n 1550, Elisabeth's father, Henry, began negotiations for her marriage to Edward VI of England.[3] This arrangement brought condemnation from Pope Julius III who reportedly stated that he would excommunicate both[a] if they marrie
2 CONC d. Henry, undeterred, agreed to a dowry of 200,000 écus, which became irrelevant upon Edward's death in 1553.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth married Philip II of Spain ("Philip the Catholic"), son of Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor and Isabella of Portugal in 1559 in Guadalajara, Spain. The marriage was a consequence of the Peace of Cateau Cambrésis (1559). His
2 CONC  second wife, Mary I of England had recently died; making Elisabeth of Valois, Philip's third wife. Elisabeth had originally been betrothed to Philip's son, Don Carlos, but political complications unexpectedly necessitated her mar
2 CONC riage to Philip instead. Despite the significant age difference between them, Philip was very attached to Elisabeth, staying close by her side even when she was ill with smallpox. Elisabeth's first pregnancy in 1564 ended with a m
2 CONC iscarriage of twin girls. She later gave birth to Infanta Isabella Clara Eugenia of Spain on 12 August 1566, and then to Isabella's younger sister Catherine Michelle of Spain October 10, 1567. Elisabeth had another miscarriage on 
2 CONC October 3, 1568, and died the same day, along with her newborn infant son.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Infanta Isabella Clara Eugenia became the major vehicle of her father's unsuccessful claims to the thrones of the Kingdom of England and France. Philip married her to her Habsburg cousin, Archduke Albert (1559-1621), who was m
2 CONC ade regent of the Spanish Netherlands in 1598. Their joint reign coincided with a golden age for the Habsburg Netherlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella bore Albert three children, Archdukes Philip (born 21 October 1605) and Albert (born 27 January 1607) and Archduchess Anna Mauritia; however, all three died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Albrecht died, the archduchess ruled as a governor in the name of her nephew, the king of Spain, for the last twelve years of her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Catarina Micaela, Élisabeth's younger daughter, was married to Carlo Emanuele I, Duke of Savoy, and was the mother of Vittorio Amedeo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sofonisba Anguissola, the first Italian woman artist to win international recognition, served as Élisabeth's lady-in-waiting from 1559 to 1569, and painted Élisabeth's portrait.
2 CONT
2 CONT Friedrich Schiller's romantic tragedy linking Don Carlos of Spain with Élisabeth of Valois in a doomed romance (without historical basis) furnished the subject of Giuseppe Verdi's Don Carlos.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were the subjects of an exhibition in Brussels in 1998-1999 [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
