1 NOTE Madame Duchess Louise of Savoy and Angouleme - Queen mother of Francis I,
2 CONT reigned over the French provinces of Bourbonnais, Beaujolais, Auvergne, la
2 CONT Marche, Angoumois, Maine, and Anjou, all of which returned to the crown
2 CONT after her death in 1531.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise of Savoy, Queen Mother of Francis I, was Regent of France in 1515 and again in 1524 to 1526 while Francis was on expeditions to Italy.  She also signed the Treat of Cambrai with Margaret of Austria in 1529, and the "Ladies 
2 CONC Peace" (Paix des Dames).
2 CONT
2 CONT When the treaty of Cambrea (Cambray) was signed 15 August 1529, the two main principals signing the treaty were mother and daughter. Both in power, at the time, in France and for the Netherlands.  Margaret of Austria, regent for C
2 CONC harles or Karl V of Germany, and her daughter, Louise of Savoy, mother of Francis I of France.  Two of Louise grandsons were being held for ransom.  These two woman used the happenstance of their power to sign the "Paix des Dames"
2 CONC  (peace of the women), which forcedFrancis to agree to give up Flanders and Artois, pay the ransom for his sons, give up Venice, and marry Eleanor, sister of Emperor Charles V (Karl V).
1 NOTE Elle est belle et intelligente mais aussi intrigante et avide. Son fils, Franðcois Ier, lui confie la Râegence pendant l'expâedition d'Italie de 1515 et apráes Pavie en 1525.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elle joue un rãole important dans la conclusion de la Paix des Dames en 1529 avec Marguerite d'Autriche.
2 CONT
2 CONT Femme de lettres âegalement, elle a laissâe des "Mâemoires".
