2 CONT
2 CONT F, #112037, b. 24 January 1547, d. 10 April 1578
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/LB88-KZF
2 CONT
2 CONT '''  Joanna Erzherzogin von Österreich''' was born on 24 January 1547 at Prague, Czech Republic.2,1 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of '''Ferdinand I von Habsburg, Holy Roman Emperor''' and '''Anna Jagellon'''.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT She married '''Francesco I de Medici, Granduca di Toscana''', son of Cosimo I de Medici, Granduca di Toscana and Eleanora de Toledo, on 18 December 1565 at Florence, Italy.3,1 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on 10 April 1578 at age 31 at Florence, Italy.2,1
2 CONT
2 CONT      Joanna Erzherzogin von Österreich was a member of the House of Habsburg. She gained the title of Erzherzogin von Österreich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Joanna Erzherzogin von Österreich and Francesco I de Medici, Granduca di Toscana
2 CONT
2 CONT # Eleonora de Medici+ b. 28 Feb 1567, d. 19 Sep 1611 (4) 
2 CONT
2 CONT # Marie de Medici+ b. 26 Apr 1573, d. 3 Jul 1642 (5)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Links===
2 CONT :
2 CONT
2 CONT The Peerage:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p11204.htm#i112037
2 CONT
2 CONT Geneall: http://www.geneall.net/D/per_page.php?id=10404
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT English: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joanna_of_Austria,_Grand_Duchess_of_Tuscany
2 CONT
2 CONT Deutsch: http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johanna_von_%C3%96sterreich
2 CONT
2 CONT Italiano: http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanna_d'Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johanna_of_Austria]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joanna of Austria, Grand Duchess of Tuscany
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure 	1574 - 1578
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Francesco I de' Medici
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Issue'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleonora, Duchess of Mantua
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie, Queen of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna de' Medici
2 CONT
2 CONT Filippo de' Medici
2 CONT
2 CONT Joanna of Austria (24 January 1547 – 11 April 1578) was the youngest daughter of Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor and Anna of Bohemia and Hungary. By marriage, she was a Grand Duchess of Tuscany; one of her daughters was the famous
2 CONC  Marie de Medici, Queen-consort and second wife of King Henry IV of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Her marriage to Francesco I de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany, took place on 18 December 1565 in Florence, after she solemnly arrived in the city by the Porta al Prato. Giorgio Vasari and Vincenzo Borghini, with the help of Giovan
2 CONC ni Caccini made big festivities for these event.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, Joanna was homesick and unhappy. Ignored by her husband, and despised by the Florentines for her Austrian hauteur, she never felt at home in Florence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father-in-law, Cosimo I de' Medici, was reasonably kind to Joanna. He had the courtyard of the Palazzo Vecchio specially decorated for her; the lunettes were painted with murals of Austrian towns by pupils of Vasari, and Verro
2 CONC cchio's Putto with Dolphin fountain was brought down from the Careggi villa where it had been set up in the garden by Lorenzo de' Medici.
2 CONT
2 CONT The position of Joanna in the Florentine court was during most of her marriage, difficult: between 1566 and 1575, she gave birth to six daughters, of whom only three survived infancy. The absence of a male heir to continue the dyn
2 CONC asty was the cause of constant conflict with her husband, who preferred the company and love of his mistress Bianca Capello, who - although some call it a fraud- gave birth a son, Antonio, in 1576.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, in 1577 Joanna gave birth to the long-awaited heir, baptized Filippo in honour to the King Philip II of Spain, Joanna's first cousin. The birth was celebrated with great joy by all the court, because was secured the succe
2 CONC ssion of the Grand Duchy for another generation and eliminated all the hopes of Bianca Capello to made her "son" Antonio as heir of Tuscany. At the end, was Johanna's brother-in-law, Ferdinando, who succeeded Francesco as Grand Du
2 CONC ke.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Death
2 CONT
2 CONT On 10 April 1578, Joanna - heavily pregnant with her eighth child - fell from the stairs in the Grand Ducal Palace in Florence. Some hours later, she gave birth to a son, who, born prematurely, died immediately. She died the next 
2 CONC day on 11 April, she also died. Francesco subsequently married his mistress, Bianca Cappello.
2 CONT
2 CONT The mysterious circumstances around these accident caused rumours accusing her husband and his mistress of murdering Joanna, so that they could be married.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT The seven children of Francesco and Johanna were:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Eleonora (b. 28 February 1567 – d. 9 September 1611), who married Vincenzo I Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua (1582-1612).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Romola (b. 20 November 1568 – d. 2 December 1568).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anna (b. 31 December 1569 – d. 19 February 1584).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Isabella (b. 30 September 1571 – d. 8 August 1572).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Lucrezia (b. 7 November 1572 – d. 14 August 1574).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Maria (b. 26 April 1575 – d. 3 July 1642), who became Queen of France by her marriage to Henri IV in 1600.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Filippo (b. 20 May 1577 – d. 29 March 1582).
2 CONT
2 CONT Out of a total of seven children, only two daughters, Eleonora and Marie lived to adulthood, the rest of the children died young. Anna the second eldest daughter died at the age of fourteen.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
