1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francesco_Maria_I_Della_Rovere
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Francesco Maria I della Rovere''' (22 March 1490 – 20 October 1538) was an Italian condottiero, who was Duke of Urbino from 1508 until 1538.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Senigallia, the son of the Papal captain and lord of that city, Giovanni della Rovere, and of Giovanna da Montefeltro, daughter of Federico III da Montefeltro. He was also the nephew of Giuliano della Rovere, pope a
2 CONC s Julius II from 1503.
2 CONT
2 CONT His uncle Guidobaldo I of Urbino, who was heirless, called him at his court, and named him as heir of that dukedom in 1504 through the intercession of Julius II. In 1502 the della Rovere had lost the seigniory of Senigallia, occup
2 CONC ied by Cesare Borgia, then the most powerful figure in the Marche: Francesco Maria and his mother were saved from the slaughter perpetrated by Borgia's troops by the then-land soldier Andrea Doria. When in 1508 Guidobaldo died, Fr
2 CONC ancesco Maria became duke of Urbino; thanks to the support of his uncle Giuliano della Rovere, now pope as Julius II, he could also recover Senigallia after Borgia's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1508 he married Eleonora Gonzaga (1493–1570), daughter of Francesco II Gonzaga, Marquess of Mantua and Isabella d'Este.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1509 he was appointed as capitano generale (commander-in-chief) of the Papal States, and subsequently fought in the Italian Wars against Ferrara and Venice. In 1511, after he had failed to conquer Bologna, he had the cardinal F
2 CONC rancesco Alidosi killed by his troops, a cruel action for which he was compared to Borgia himself. In 1513 he was created also lord of Pesaro.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the death of Julius II deprived him of his main political patron, and under the new pope, Leo X Medici, Pesaro was given to the latter's nephew, Lorenzo II de' Medici. In 1516 he was excommunicated and ousted from Urbino,
2 CONC  which he tried unsuccessfully to recover the following year (see War of Urbino). He could return in his duchy only after Leo's death (1521).
2 CONT
2 CONT Della Rovere fought as capitano generale of the Republic of Venice in Lombardy during the Italian Wars of 1521 (1523–1525), but with the new Medici Pope, Clement VII, the della Rovere were increasingly marginalized. As supreme com
2 CONC mander of the Holy League, his inaction against the Imperial invasion troops is generally listed as one of the causes of the Sack of Rome (1527).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a protagonist of the capture of Pavia in the late 1520s, and later fought for the Republic of Venice. Later he arranged the marriage of son Guidobaldo to Giulia da Varano (belonging to another former seigniory family of the
2 CONC  region) to counter the Papal power in the Marche.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Pesaro, poisoned. Some scholars suggest that The Murder of Gonzago, an unknown play referenced in William Shakespeare's Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, which is itself later reworked by Hamlet into The Mousetrap (the play wi
2 CONC thin the play), may have been a popular theatrical reenactment of Della Rovere's death and may have been portrayed in England's early theaters during the Elizabethan Era.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francesco_Maria_I_della_Rovere,_Duke_of_Urbino#Issue Issue]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Giulia della Rovere (died 1563) married Alfonso d'Este, Lord of Montecchio and had issue (were parents of Cesare d'Este, Duke of Modena);
2 CONT
2 CONT * Elisabetta della Rovere (died 6 June 1561) married Alberico I Cybo-Malaspina, Marquis of Massa and had issue (ancestors of Maria Teresa Cybo-Malaspina);
2 CONT
2 CONT * Guidobaldo della Rovere (2 April 1514 - 28 September 1574) married Giulia Varano and had issue; married Vittoria Farnese (daughter of Pier Luigi Farnese, Duke of Parma) had issue (ancestors of Maria Teresa Cybo-Malaspina);
2 CONT
2 CONT * Giulio della Rovere (1533–1578) became a cardinal then later had issue (illegitimate); Ippolito and Giulio.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ippolita della Rovere (b.1525) had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT * ... .. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * ... ..
