1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Sommaire=
2 CONT * Marquis de Montferrat :
2 CONT ** précédé de [http://www.geni.com/people/Gian-Giacomo-Palaiologos/6000000008519893788 Jean-Jacques]
2 CONT ** succédé par [http://www.geni.com/people/Guglielmo-VIII-Paleologo/6000000015319898890 Guillaume VIII]
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT * [http://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000027609064898 Moreri, L., ''Le Grand Dictionnaire Historique -- Les Montferrat'', 1759] (ci-joint)
2 CONT * [http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marchesato_del_Monferrato#Periodo_Paleologo Marchesato del Monferrato, Wikipédia]
2 CONT * [http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_IV_del_Monferrato Giovanni IV del Monferrato, Wikipédia]
2 CONT
2 CONT ====================
2 CONT
2 CONT John IV of Montferrat
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT John IV Palaeologus (Italian: Giovanni IV Paleologo; June 24, 1413 - January 19, 1464) was the Margrave of Montferrat from 1445 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The eldest of four brothers and two sisters, he was born near Casale in the castle of Pontestura, to John Jacob of Montferrat and Joan of Savoy, daughter of Amadeus VII (the Red Count of Savoy).[1] During his father’s unlucky war 
2 CONC against Amadeus VIII of Savoy, he was imprisoned by the latter and used as hostage.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought as condottiere for the Republic of Venice in the war that ensued after the heirless death of Filippo Maria Visconti of Milan (see Wars in Lombardy). During his reign the Palaeologus family lost the throne of Constantinop
2 CONC le, captured by the Ottoman Turks in 1453.
2 CONT
2 CONT He set his mind rather belatedly to ensuring future of the dynasty, marrying Margherita, daughter of Louis of Savoy and Anne of Cyprus, in Casale in December 1458. She brought a dowry of 100,000 scudi, receiving Trino, Morano, Bor
2 CONC go San Martino and Mombaruzzo in return. However they had no children.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT John IV died, without legitimate heirs, in Casale on 19 January 1464; he was buried there alongside his father in the church of San Francesco.[1] He was succeeded by his brother William VIII.
