1 NOTE Charles VIII, called the Affable, son of Louis XI, succeeded his father to the throne of France in 1483.  His reign ended in 1498 when he was killed in a fall from his horse.  He was succeeded by. Louis XII, his brother-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles VIII of France became heir to Provence in 1483 and was the last Count of Provense; and the territory then became a part of France, and was later divided into the departments of Bouches-du-Rhone; Var, and Basses-Alpes, and 
2 CONC parts of the departments of Drome, Alpes-maritimes; and Vaucluse in 1790.
1 NOTE Charles VIII (of France) (1470-98), king of France (1483-98). He was the 
2 CONT son of Louis XI. His sister Anne (1461-1522) served as regent from 1483 to 
2 CONT 1491, when he began to reign in his own right. By his marriage (1491) to 
2 CONT Anne of Brittany (1477-1514), that province was united to France. The 
2 CONT chief event of his reign was his invasion of Italy in 1494 and his 
2 CONT temporary occupation (1495) of Naples. As a result of that invasion and 
2 CONT several subsequent ones, Italian cultural influences were introduced into 
2 CONT France.
1 NOTE Charles VIII (1470-98), King of France, succeeded in 1483 his father, Louis XI. For eight years his sister Anne practically ruled France with a strong hand, repelling foreign invaders, while giving peace and prosperity at home. Ch
2 CONC arles, however, chafed under her control, and dreamed of conquering Italy, and of expelling the Turks from Europe; but when he invaded Italy in 1495 he so alienated the people by his imprudence and thoughtlessness that they threat
2 CONC ened to cut off his retreat. However, he gained a decisive victory at Fornova (1495). By his marriage to the heiress of the Duke of Brittany he added Brittany to France. See Cherrier�s Histoire de Charles VIII. [World Wide Illustr
2 CONC ated Encyclopedia, 1935]
1 NOTE KING OF FRANCE 1483-1498 (ACCEDED 8/30/1483, CROWNED 5/14/1484); DIED AS A
2 CONT RESULT OF HITTING HIS HEAD
1 NOTE Jusqu'en 1491, en raison de son jeune ãage, il laisse la râegence áa sa soeur ainâee, Anne de Beaujeu, qui lutte contre les nobles râevoltâes et obtient la victoire de Saint-Aubin-du-Cormier en 1488
2 CONT sur le duc d'Orlâeans (futur Louis XII) et le duc de Bretagne Franðcois II. Anne de Beaujeu le marie áa Anne de Bretagne et prâepare ainsi le rattachement du duchâe áa la couronne de France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Affranchi de la tutelle de sa soeur, il  signe les traitâes d'Etaples avec Henri VII d'Angleterre et de Senlis avec Maximilien d'Autriche áa qui il restitue l'Artois et la Franche-Comtâe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il entreprend ensuite de faire valoir ses droits sur la maison de Naples, hâeritâes par Louis XI de la maison d'Anjou (voir Renâe d'Anjou).
2 CONT
2 CONT Il gagne facilement le royaume de Naples en 1495 mais Milan, Venise, Maximilien d'Autriche, Ferdinand d'Aragon et le pape Alexandre VI se liguent contre lui et il doit battre en retraite. Il
2 CONT doit remporter la victoire de Fornoue qui lui permet de regagner la France, en abandonnant toutes ses conquãetes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dâecâedâe sans postâerite mãale, le jeune dauphin Charles Orland âetant mort áa l'ãage de 3 ans, la succession de Charles VIII est prâevue en la personne du duc d'Orlâeans Louis, arriáere-petit-fils de
2 CONT Charles V.
