2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel was the second child and eldest daughter of Manuel I of Portugal and his second wife, Infanta Maria of Castile and Aragon. She was named after her maternal grandmother, Isabella I of Castile and her aunt Isabella, Princess 
2 CONC of Asturias, who had been her father's first wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her father, she was a granddaughter of Infante Ferdinand, Duke of Viseu (the second son of king Edward of Portugal and brother of Afonso V of Portugal) and Infanta Beatrice, the daughter of Infante João, Lord of Reguengos 
2 CONC (brother of king Edward). Through her mother she was a granddaughter of Isabella I of Castile and Ferdinand II of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel was second-in-line to the throne until the birth of her brother Louis in 1505. However, as the oldest daughter of Manuel I of Portugal, she was a rather attractive party. She married her first cousin, Charles V, Holy Roman 
2 CONC Emperor, the son of Joanna of Castile and Philip the Handsome, Duke of Burgundy, who as Holy Roman Emperor, King of Aragon and Castile, Archduke of the Habsburg dominions, titular Duke of Burgundy, and ruler of the Netherlands and
2 CONC  the Spanish empire in the Americas and the Mediterranean and Italy was one of the most powerful men of his time.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1521 Isabel's father died and her brother succeeded to the throne as king John III. The marriage negotiations between the Portuguese and Spanish began almost immediately. It was agreed that the new king would marry Catherine of
2 CONC  Austria, Charles V's younger sister. The union between Charles and Isabel only took place three years later, by procuration in 1525. The Infanta travelled to Seville where the wedding took place on 11 March 1526. With Isabel also
2 CONC  came a huge dowry to the Spanish finances. Although it was a political union, the marriage proved to be a love-match. Records say that during their honeymoon "when [Charles and Isabel] are together, although there are many people
2 CONC  around, they do not notice anyone else; they talk and laugh, and nothing else distracts them."
2 CONT
2 CONT Marble statue of Isabella of Portugal by Pompeo Leoni (1572, Prado, Madrid)Isabel also proved to be a competent consort; she served as regent of Spain during her husband's absences, between 1529-1532 and 1535-1539. She was noted f
2 CONC or her intelligence and beauty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel died in 1539 after the birth of her sixth child. The Emperor was away at the time and her premature death affected him deeply. He never remarried, and he dressed in black for the rest of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1547, the nobleman Francis Borgia conveyed her corpse to her burial-place in Granada. It is said that, when he saw the effect of death on the beautiful empress, he decided to "never again serve a mortal master", later becoming 
2 CONC a Catholic saint.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1580, more than 40 years after her death, her son Philip succeeded the Portuguese throne, claiming his mother's successory rights.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel married Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor on 10 March 1526. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip II of Spain (1527 - 1598), King of Spain and Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Spain (1528-1603), married her cousin Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand (1530)
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella (?) (1536-1538), died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of Spain (1535-1573), married her cousin Prince John of Portugal and was the mother of king Sebastian of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT John (1539)
2 CONT
