2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Austria coat of arms as queen of FranceBorn in Louvain as the eldest child of Philip the Handsome, archduke of Austria and Duke of Burgundy and of Joanna of Castile, Infanta of Castile and Aragon and later Queen of the 
2 CONC named countries. Her siblings were: Charles V of the Holy Roman Empire, Ferdinand I, also Emperor of the Holy Roman Empire, Elisabeth Queen of Denmark, Mary Queen of Hungary and later Regent of the Low Countries and Catherine, Que
2 CONC en of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT When she was young, her relatives tried to marry her to the King of England, Henry VIII), whom she was betrothed to but when his father died and he became King he decided to marry her aunt Catherine of Aragon with whom he had been
2 CONC  in love with for a number of years prior to their marriage. They also tried to marry her off to the French King (Louis XII or Francis I) or with the Polish King (Sigismund I), but they could not. Finally they married her to Manue
2 CONC l I of Portugal, after the proposed marriage with her cousin (the Crown Prince) did not occur. They married 16 July 1518. They had two children: the Infante Charles (who died as a child, born 18 February 1520) and the Infanta Mari
2 CONC a (born 8 June 1521, and who was later one of the richest Princesses of Europe). She became a widow on 13 December, 1521, when Manuel died of the plague.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time later as a widow, by the treaty called "La Paz de las Damas" (The Ladies' Peace) she was married to Francis I of France on 4 July 1530. They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT As the French Queen she did not have any political power; however, she was used as a contact between France and the Holy Roman Empire. She was Charles V's favorite sister. She died in 1558 on the return trip from Badajoz, where sh
2 CONC e tried to make peace with her daughter Maria.
2 CONT
