1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Gismonda [di Antonio di Messer Palmieri] Altoviti was a 15th-century Florentine noblewoman recorded in civic and burial records (Sepoltuario) as active in 1433 or later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lineage & Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Paternal Lineage: Daughter of Antonio Altoviti; paternal granddaughter of Messer Palmieri di Arnaldo Altoviti (14th-century ambassador and Priore) and his wife Gismonda degli Alberti or Lisa Sapiti.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: Married Bernardo di Bartolo di Messer Bindo Bardi, son of Bartolomeo Bardi and Filippa Tornaquinci.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: No issue recorded in primary encyclopedia index files for this specific union.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources
2 CONT * https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/palmieri-altoviti_res-2fd8d717-87e6-11dc-8e9d-0016357eee51_(Dizionario-Biografico)/ (does not list descendants)
2 CONT * https://sepoltuario.iath.virginia.edu/individuals/9758 cites 
2 CONT * ASF, Manoscritti, Carte Pucci	ins. 593/II, 23
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altoviti
2 CONT * Prajda, Katalin. ''Network and Migration in Early Renaissance Florence, 1378–1433: Friends of Friends in the Kingdom of Hungary.'' Amsterdam: Amsterdam University Press, 2018. JSTOR,  < [https://www.jstor.org/content/pdf/oa_book
2 CONC _monograph/j.ctvfp63mt PDF] > Prajda includes the Altoviti under her analysis of elite Florentine family structures and diplomatic presence. She examines figures such as '''Leonardo and Martino di Caccia Altoviti''', highlighting 
2 CONC how the diplomatic prestige established by 14th-century figures (like '''Messer Palmieri Altoviti''') translated into generational political authority, strategic marriages, and military financing during wars against the Visconti.
