0 @N6000000002188342462@ NOTE Edward VI of England
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT "Edward Tudor" redirects here. For the impostor who masqueraded as Edward Plantagenet, 17th Earl of Warwick, and as such claimed the throne as "Edward VI" in 1487, see Lambert Simnel.
1 CONT This article needs additional citations for verification.
1 CONT Please help improve this article by adding reliable references. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (March 2008)
1 CONT Edward VI
1 CONT King of England and Ireland (more...)
1 CONT Edward as Prince of Wales, Flemish School
1 CONT Edward as Prince of Wales, Flemish School[1]
1 CONT Reign 28 January 1547 – 6 July 1553
1 CONT Coronation 20 February 1547
1 CONT Predecessor Henry VIII
1 CONT Regent Lord Northumberland (1549–1553)
1 CONT Lord Somerset (1547–1549)
1 CONT and Council
1 CONT Successor Jane de facto;
1 CONT Mary I de jure
1 CONT Detail
1 CONT Titles and styles
1 CONT HM The King
1 CONT The Prince of Wales
1 CONT The Duke of Cornwall
1 CONT Royal house House of Tudor
1 CONT Father Henry VIII
1 CONT Mother Jane Seymour
1 CONT Born 12 October 1537(1537-10-12)
1 CONT Hampton Court Palace, Richmond upon Thames
1 CONT Died 6 July 1553 (aged 15)
1 CONT Palace of Placentia, Greenwich
1 CONT Burial 9 August 1553
1 CONT Henry VII Lady Chapel, Westminster Abbey
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward VI (12 October 1537 – 6 July 1553) became King of England and Ireland, on 28 January 1547, and was crowned on 20 February, at ten years of age. He also carried the English claim to the French throne, but he did not rule Fra
1 CONC nce. Edward, the son of Henry VIII and Jane Seymour, was the third monarch of the Tudor dynasty and England's first born Protestant ruler. Edward's entire rule was mediated through a council of regency, as he never reached maturit
1 CONC y. The council was first led by his uncle, Edward Seymour, 1st Duke of Somerset (1547–1549), and then by John Dudley, 1st Duke of Northumberland (1549–1553).
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Henry VIII had severed the link between the English church and Rome, it was during Edward's reign that Protestantism was established for the first time in England, with Thomas Cranmer, the Archbishop of Canterbury, implem
1 CONC enting the Book of Common Prayer. Edward's reign was marked by increasingly harsh Protestant reforms, the loss of control of Scotland, and an economic downturn. A period of social unrest begun earlier intensified during his rule, 
1 CONC and conflicts with the French increased.
1 CONT
1 CONT When it became clear that Edward's life was to be a short one, the Device to Alter the Succession was drafted. This made Lady Jane Grey, Edward's solidly Protestant cousin, the first in line of succession to the throne by excludin
1 CONC g his two half sisters, the devout Catholic Mary and moderate Protestant Elizabeth. Following Edward's death at the age of fifteen, a disputed succession reopened the religious conflicts. Lady Jane was Queen for only nine days, du
1 CONC ring that time reigning in name only, before she was replaced by Mary. Queen Mary then sought to undo many of Edward's Protestant reforms with the Marian Repeal Acts in her first two Parliaments.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
