1 CONC ough cunning, diplomacy underminded many of the sound economic and political achievements of the previous decade.  His goal to incorporate Geneva, Saluzzo, and Montferrat into Savoy caused him to oscillate in his alliances between
1 CONC  France and Spain. In the long run he met only limited success. 1602 he tried unsuccessfully to reconquer Geneva by surprise attack.  He recovered Saluzzo from the French by the treaty of Lyons (1601), giving up in exchange, Bress
1 CONC e, Bugey, Gex, and Pinerolo, but he lost Saluzzo just before his death.  He waged war over the succession to Montferrat for much of the first quarter of the 16th century. At the time of his death his duchy was overrun by the Frenc
